### TITLE ###
ConstraintsMatchingTheirArgTypeAreFine
### DESCRIPTION ###
The ordinary case: a list takes the same constraints as the type it holds,
because the constraint describes each element rather than the list.
### INPUT ###
args:
    envs str[]
    scores int[]
    tags str[]
    envs enum ["dev", "prd"]
    scores range [0, 100]
    tags regex "^[a-z]+$"
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  (none)

# Symbol types
  envs (arg): str[]
  scores (arg): int[]
  tags (arg): str[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
ConstraintThatCannotApplyIsReported
### DESCRIPTION ###
These declarations used to parse, reach the arg parser and be dropped there, so
a script looked like it validated its input and did not. Reporting them is the
point of RAD40024 - and it catches the scalar spelling too, which had the same
silent drop.
### INPUT ###
args:
    scores int[]
    names str[]
    age int
    scores enum ["a", "b"]
    names range [0, 10]
    age regex "[0-9]+"
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  (none)

# Symbol types
  age (arg): int
  names (arg): str[]
  scores (arg): int[]

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD40024 @ 5:5 - 'scores' is int, so an enum constraint cannot apply
    help: Enum constraints need a str arg.
  [error] RAD40024 @ 6:5 - 'names' is str, so a range constraint cannot apply
    help: Range constraints need an int or float arg.
  [error] RAD40024 @ 7:5 - 'age' is int, so a regex constraint cannot apply
    help: Regex constraints need a str arg.
